Found by the Perl-CLI gap scout (2026-11-13), verified by hand probe: `node dist/skilletor.js hook session-start` run from a cwd that no longer exists prints `skilletor: Error: ENOENT: process.cwd failed … uv_cwd` plus a stack trace on stderr and exits 1.

Cause: run() calls makeContext → projectRootOf(process.cwd()) (src/cli.ts:231) before the hook branch and outside its try (:233). That breaks the hook contract (spec §8: a hook never fails, exits 0), and every hook call spawns a needless `git rev-parse` (the hook path takes its project from SKILLETOR_PROJECT_DIR / CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR / the payload cwd).

Done: the hook branch builds its context inside the exit-0 wrapper (no rev-parse it does not need); a black-box test through the CLI from a removed cwd exits 0, silent or with a systemMessage; dist rebuilt. The Perl CLI (strand card) mirrors the fixed behaviour.

Same contract, second hole (conformance scout, read from the code, JS behaviour confirmed with a minimal snippet): cli.ts:340 'return runHookCommand(...)' without await inside the try, so a rejection skips the catch at :345; homedir() at :380 sits outside the inner try (:390). A throw there exits 1 with a stack (:415-421). Fix both with the deleted-cwd case.

2026-11-13 20:48 Reproduced by hand (temp HOME/CODEX_HOME, cwd removed under sh): dist hook session-start prints 'skilletor: Error: ENOENT: process.cwd failed … uv_cwd' + stack from makeContext2, exit 1. Plan: hook dispatch moves ahead of makeContext and the --scope check; runHookCommand wraps its whole body (homedir included) in the exit-0 catch, so it never rejects. Tests in test/cli.test.ts: removed cwd via sh, an os.homedir fault via --import preload (pins the second hole), a fake git on PATH (no rev-parse when the project is known).

